---
import SymmetryBackground from './SymmetryBackground.astro';
import SectionMark from './SectionMark.astro';
import Glyph from './Glyph.astro';
import CalibrationBar from './CalibrationBar.astro';
import ValenceCurve from './ValenceCurve.astro';
import ResearchReferences from './ResearchReferences.astro';
---

<!-- ── Header ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="relative pt-20 pb-12 border-b border-csr-gray-200 overflow-hidden">
  <SymmetryBackground variant="lattice" opacity={0.08} />
  <div class="relative max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
      <SectionMark />
      <p class="label-mono">Long-Term Research Agenda</p>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight text-csr-black mb-6 max-w-2xl leading-tight">
      Five open questions on internal model states
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-csr-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl font-serif mb-6">
      This agenda outlines our long-term theoretical framework: five questions spanning measurement, mechanism, intervention, and substrate neutrality.
    </p>

    <!-- Prominent note about reproductions focus -->
    <div class="glass-card rounded-xl p-4 sm:p-5 border border-csr-primary/20 bg-csr-primary/5 flex items-start gap-3">
      <div class="text-csr-primary shrink-0 mt-0.5 font-bold">ℹ</div>
      <p class="text-sm text-csr-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-sans">
        <strong>Current Focus:</strong> This page describes our long-term research direction. The active day-to-day focus of CSR is reproducing published mechanistic interpretability results. See <a href="/reproductions/" class="text-csr-primary font-semibold hover:underline">Reproductions</a> for current write-ups.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── Sticky Sub-nav ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="sticky top-[73px] z-40 bg-csr-cream/95 backdrop-blur border-b border-csr-gray-200">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-3 flex items-center gap-4 sm:gap-6 text-xs font-mono overflow-x-auto">
    <a href="#mission" class="text-csr-gray-600 hover:text-csr-primary whitespace-nowrap">Q1 · Mission</a>
    <a href="#measurement" class="text-csr-gray-600 hover:text-csr-primary whitespace-nowrap">Q2 · Measurement</a>
    <a href="#mechanisms" class="text-csr-gray-600 hover:text-csr-primary whitespace-nowrap">Q3 · Mechanisms</a>
    <a href="#intervention" class="text-csr-gray-600 hover:text-csr-primary whitespace-nowrap">Q4 · Intervention</a>
    <a href="#substrate" class="text-csr-gray-600 hover:text-csr-primary whitespace-nowrap">Q5 · Substrate</a>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 space-y-24">

  <!-- ── Q1: Mission & Theory of Change ───────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="mission" class="scroll-mt-36">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
      <SectionMark />
      <p class="label-mono">Q1 · Mission & Theory of Change</p>
    </div>
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-csr-black mb-6">
      What future are we working toward, and through what causal chain?
    </h2>
    <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none font-serif text-lg leading-relaxed text-csr-gray-700 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        Our mission is understanding the physical and computational conditions for positive and negative states across biological and digital substrates. We take seriously the possibility that scaled computation could one day support states with valence, and we want diagnostic tools to recognize those states to exist before the question becomes urgent rather than after.
      </p>
      <p>
        That commitment only becomes research if it terminates in something measurable. The research agenda traces four stages: measurement (Q2), mechanism and diagnostics (Q3), biological calibration (Q5), and governance.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="glass-card rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 border border-csr-gray-200 mb-8">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-csr-black mb-4">
        The causal chain
      </h3>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-sm font-serif">
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/60 border border-csr-gray-100">
          <p class="font-sans font-semibold text-xs text-csr-primary mb-1">1. Microscale mapping (input)</p>
          <p class="text-csr-gray-600">Defining geometric invariants of MLP microcircuits and feature density.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/60 border border-csr-gray-100">
          <p class="font-sans font-semibold text-xs text-csr-primary mb-1">2. Macroscale diagnostics (process)</p>
          <p class="text-csr-gray-600">Using Jacobian and spectral metrics to map global state-space trajectories.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/60 border border-csr-gray-100">
          <p class="font-sans font-semibold text-xs text-csr-primary mb-1">3. Biological calibration (output)</p>
          <p class="text-csr-gray-600">Cross-referencing metrics with biological electrophysiology and connectome data as a plausibility check.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/60 border border-csr-gray-100">
          <p class="font-sans font-semibold text-xs text-csr-primary mb-1">4. Welfare governance (impact)</p>
          <p class="text-csr-gray-600">Providing non-verbal, objective markers that do not rely on easily gamified model self-reports.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Q2: Measurement ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="measurement" class="scroll-mt-36">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
      <SectionMark />
      <p class="label-mono">Q2 · Measurement</p>
    </div>
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-csr-black mb-6">
      What can we reliably quantify and detect?
    </h2>
    <p class="text-lg text-csr-gray-700 font-serif leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Before interpreting anything, we need stable, basis-invariant quantities. Verbal self-reports are easily gamified, so we evaluate how a model's internal activity and representational geometry are organized across two distinct scales:
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-8">
      <div class="glass-card rounded-2xl p-6 border border-csr-gray-200">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-csr-black mb-2">Microscale: feature geometry</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-csr-gray-600 font-serif leading-relaxed">
          Feature geometry in MLP microcircuits: singular value spectrum entropy, trace, Orthogonal Dissonance (how far a transformation departs from angle-preserving), and distances to Lie groups mapped across layers.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="glass-card rounded-2xl p-6 border border-csr-gray-200">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-csr-black mb-2">Macroscale: dynamical state space</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-csr-gray-600 font-serif leading-relaxed">
          Dynamical state space and attractor stability traced via local Jacobians, revealing trajectory dynamics and phase transitions across deep layers.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="glass-card rounded-2xl p-6 border border-csr-gray-200 text-sm font-serif text-csr-gray-700">
      <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-xs text-csr-primary uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Symmetry characterization</h3>
      <p>
        We extract MLP weights from open-source LLMs and construct the local, state-dependent transformation operator A(x) = W₂ · D(x) · W₁, then compute basis-invariant metrics across layers to track how learning shapes singular-value structure.<sup><a href="#ref-4" class="text-csr-primary hover:underline">[4]</a></sup>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Q3: Mechanisms ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="mechanisms" class="scroll-mt-36">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
      <SectionMark />
      <p class="label-mono">Q3 · Mechanisms</p>
    </div>
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-csr-black mb-6">
      How does structure relate to valence, and what does it teach interpretability?
    </h2>
    <p class="text-lg text-csr-gray-700 font-serif leading-relaxed mb-8">
      A metric is only useful if it is functionally involved in what the model computes, rather than being an incidental byproduct of training. These hypotheses formulate falsifiable relationships:
    </p>

    <div class="space-y-6 mb-8">
      <div class="glass-card rounded-2xl p-6 border border-csr-gray-200">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-csr-primary font-semibold">Hypothesis A</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-csr-gray-400">Microscale</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-csr-black mb-2">Functional Coherence</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-csr-gray-700 font-serif leading-relaxed mb-4">
          High-dimensional feature geometry in MLP microcircuits (measured by low Orthogonal Dissonance and high Spectral Entropy) is functionally involved in encoding meaning and value. We test whether Global Workspace Theory (GWT)-style broadcasting and binding manifest as geometric symmetries in the feature space.<sup><a href="#ref-2" class="text-csr-primary hover:underline">[2]</a></sup>
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs font-mono text-csr-gray-500 bg-white/60 p-3 rounded-lg border border-csr-gray-100">
          <strong>Falsification condition:</strong> Falsified if targeted, symmetry-breaking weight perturbations result in zero task performance or consistency degradation under preference probes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="glass-card rounded-2xl p-6 border border-csr-gray-200">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-csr-primary font-semibold">Hypothesis B</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-csr-gray-400">Macroscale</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-csr-black mb-2">Attractor State Stability</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-csr-gray-700 font-serif leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Characterizing when behavioral attractors correspond to identifiable internal dynamics (such as stable, low-dimensional resonant manifolds of low Orthogonal Dissonance) versus when they reflect training-distribution artifacts.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs font-mono text-csr-gray-500 bg-white/60 p-3 rounded-lg border border-csr-gray-100">
          <strong>Falsification condition:</strong> If regularizing a model toward representational symmetry degrades baseline language capability or forces output collapse, Hypothesis B is false.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Q4: Intervention ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="intervention" class="scroll-mt-36">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
      <SectionMark />
      <p class="label-mono">Q4 · Intervention</p>
    </div>
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-csr-black mb-6">
      Can we suggest cheap interventions that do not degrade the model?
    </h2>
    <p class="text-lg text-csr-gray-700 font-serif leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Detection is limited if we cannot test causality. We test whether geometric metrics are causally steerable: whether nudging representational geometry alters internal state without unacceptable capability loss.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-8">
      <div class="glass-card rounded-2xl p-6 border border-csr-gray-200">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-csr-black mb-2">Controlled interventions</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-csr-gray-600 font-serif leading-relaxed">
          Designing perturbations that selectively break symmetry (deforming spectra) or preserve it (rotational space transforms), evaluating causal downstream effects on capability benchmarks.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="glass-card rounded-2xl p-6 border border-csr-gray-200">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-csr-black mb-2">Symmetry regularization</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-csr-gray-600 font-serif leading-relaxed">
          Testing symmetry metrics as regularizing objectives (task loss vs. orthogonal dissonance) to evaluate whether conformal representations preserve capability while avoiding representational collapse.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Q5: Substrate ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="substrate" class="scroll-mt-36">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
      <SectionMark />
      <p class="label-mono">Q5 · Substrate</p>
    </div>
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-csr-black mb-6">
      Can we extrapolate our findings to other sentient substrates?
    </h2>
    <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none font-serif text-lg leading-relaxed text-csr-gray-700 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        Substrate-neutrality is the claim that similar structural and topological signatures appear across physical media. Testing this against biological data is a longer-term question once empirical digital metrics are established; the current focus of CSR is reproducing published interpretability findings and evaluating model representations.
      </p>
      <p>
        A later-stage inquiry is cross-referencing simulated geometric metrics with invertebrate electrophysiology and connectome data (e.g. stomatogastric ganglion, <em>C. elegans</em>) as a method plausibility check. If similar geometric invariants organize measured neural dynamics in vivo, the substrate-neutral hypothesis gains ground; if not, findings remain strictly bounded to artificial networks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── References ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="py-12 border-t border-csr-gray-200 bg-csr-cream">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <p class="label-mono text-xs mb-4">References</p>
    <div class="space-y-3 text-xs text-csr-gray-500 font-mono leading-relaxed">
      <p id="ref-1">
        [1] Anthis, J. R. et al. (2025). <em>Perceptions of Sentient AI and Other Digital Minds: Evidence from the AI, Morality, and Sentience (AIMS) Survey.</em> CHI 2025. <a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3706598.3713329" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-csr-primary hover:underline break-all">https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3706598.3713329</a>
      </p>
      <p id="ref-2">
        [2] Butlin, P. et al. (2023). <em>Consciousness in Artificial Intelligence: Insights from the Science of Consciousness.</em> arXiv. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.08708" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-csr-primary hover:underline break-all">https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.08708</a>
      </p>
      <p id="ref-4">
        [4] Gurnee, W. et al. (2026). <em>Verbalizable Representations Form a Global Workspace in Language Models.</em> transformer-circuits.pub. <a href="https://transformer-circuits.pub/2026/workspace/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-csr-primary hover:underline break-all">https://transformer-circuits.pub/2026/workspace/index.html</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
